WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.100
Hi I'm Michelle and I have a blog called

00:00:02.100 --> 00:00:03.840
Lab Muffin. Today I'm going to be

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:05.910
talking about why pH matters for alpha

00:00:05.910 --> 00:00:08.490
hydroxy acids and vitamin C. There are

00:00:08.490 --> 00:00:10.139
many acidic active ingredients in

00:00:10.139 --> 00:00:12.540
skincare: alpha hydroxy acids like

00:00:12.540 --> 00:00:15.210
glycolic, lactic and malic acids; beta

00:00:15.210 --> 00:00:18.240
hydroxy acid or salicylic acid; vitamin C

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:20.850
or L-ascorbic acid; hyaluronic acid,

00:00:20.850 --> 00:00:23.789
retinoic acid and many more. You might

00:00:23.789 --> 00:00:25.260
have heard that some acid products need

00:00:25.260 --> 00:00:27.420
to be a certain pH to work. Why is that?

00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:29.640
Here's the science behind the skin care tip.

00:00:31.530 --> 00:00:34.110
Acids are molecules that can give up

00:00:34.110 --> 00:00:37.350
hydrogen ions or H+. For example, sulfuric

00:00:37.350 --> 00:00:39.180
acid or battery acid is H2SO4,

00:00:39.180 --> 00:00:42.150
hydrochloric acid in your stomach is HCl,

00:00:42.150 --> 00:00:44.730
and acetic acid in vinegar is CH3COOH.

00:00:44.730 --> 00:00:47.730
All of them can break up to give up

00:00:47.730 --> 00:00:51.150
hydrogen ions like so. You can see that

00:00:51.150 --> 00:00:53.070
the acids in skincare also have hydrogen

00:00:53.070 --> 00:00:55.820
that can break off.

00:00:56.420 --> 00:00:58.850
The top layer of the skin, the stratum

00:00:58.850 --> 00:01:00.769
corneum, is made up of cells embedded in

00:01:00.769 --> 00:01:03.379
oily lipids much like bricks in mortar.

00:01:03.379 --> 00:01:06.950
Oil is non-polar, which means it isn't

00:01:06.950 --> 00:01:09.470
charged, while water is polar, which means

00:01:09.470 --> 00:01:11.509
it's partially charged. According to the

00:01:11.509 --> 00:01:13.340
rule of thumb for solubility "like

00:01:13.340 --> 00:01:15.920
dissolves like" - that is, things that are

00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:17.479
uncharged like to mingle with

00:01:17.479 --> 00:01:19.369
things that are also uncharged, but

00:01:19.369 --> 00:01:20.899
they'll repel things that are charged.

00:01:20.899 --> 00:01:23.720
This means that in general, charged

00:01:23.720 --> 00:01:27.580
particles will not get into skin easily.

00:01:28.659 --> 00:01:30.729
When acids haven't given up their

00:01:30.729 --> 00:01:32.740
hydrogen ions they're uncharged so

00:01:32.740 --> 00:01:34.149
they're able to penetrate the stratum

00:01:34.149 --> 00:01:36.369
corneum. The uncharged version is called

00:01:36.369 --> 00:01:39.369
the free acid. But if they've broken up

00:01:39.369 --> 00:01:41.859
or dissociated into a hydrogen ion and

00:01:41.859 --> 00:01:43.899
a negatively charged anion, they're too

00:01:43.899 --> 00:01:45.159
polar to get through the non-polar

00:01:45.159 --> 00:01:48.159
stratum corneum very easily. The extent

00:01:48.159 --> 00:01:49.840
to which the acid is broken up depends

00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:51.849
on a few things. Let's talk about the two

00:01:51.849 --> 00:01:53.170
that are most important in skincare.

00:01:53.170 --> 00:01:55.780
First, the strength of the bond holding

00:01:55.780 --> 00:01:57.280
the hydrogen ion to the rest of the

00:01:57.280 --> 00:01:59.109
acid. This is measured by something

00:01:59.109 --> 00:02:01.989
called the pKa of the acid. For example

00:02:01.989 --> 00:02:05.140
hydrochloric acid has a very low pKa, so

00:02:05.140 --> 00:02:06.939
in water-based solutions it's almost

00:02:06.939 --> 00:02:09.399
100% broken up. Acetic acid on

00:02:09.399 --> 00:02:11.140
the other hand has a much higher pKa

00:02:11.140 --> 00:02:14.709
so in water only about 1 in 100 acidic

00:02:14.709 --> 00:02:17.530
hydrogens have broken off. We call this

00:02:17.530 --> 00:02:20.319
strong and weak. Skincare acids are all

00:02:20.319 --> 00:02:22.540
weak acids so they're varying amounts of

00:02:22.540 --> 00:02:25.269
broken up. Secondly there's also the pH

00:02:25.269 --> 00:02:27.970
of the overall product. pH measures the

00:02:27.970 --> 00:02:29.980
total concentration of hydrogen ions and

00:02:29.980 --> 00:02:32.650
low pH means more hydrogen. The hydrogen

00:02:32.650 --> 00:02:34.840
ions can come from the skincare acid or

00:02:34.840 --> 00:02:36.579
from other acids added to the mixture.

00:02:36.579 --> 00:02:39.459
Let's consider a product where half the

00:02:39.459 --> 00:02:41.950
acid molecules are in the free acid form so

00:02:41.950 --> 00:02:44.739
half can get into the skin. If the pH is

00:02:44.739 --> 00:02:46.720
lower, there's more hydrogen ions around,

00:02:46.720 --> 00:02:48.489
which means more will attach onto the

00:02:48.489 --> 00:02:49.989
acid molecules giving more of the

00:02:49.989 --> 00:02:51.489
uncharged free acid, which can penetrate.

00:02:51.489 --> 00:02:54.880
This means that a lower concentration of

00:02:54.880 --> 00:02:57.040
acid at a lower pH will give the

00:02:57.040 --> 00:02:58.569
equivalent amount of free acid as a

00:02:58.569 --> 00:03:01.840
higher concentration at a higher pH. For

00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:03.579
example, a product with 10%

00:03:03.579 --> 00:03:05.799
glycolic acid at pH 3 would have

00:03:05.799 --> 00:03:07.480
8.7% of the product

00:03:07.480 --> 00:03:10.269
in uncharged form, whereas at pH 4.5

00:03:10.269 --> 00:03:12.700
you'd need 50% glycolic acid

00:03:12.700 --> 00:03:16.150
to get a similar amount. In summary, low

00:03:16.150 --> 00:03:18.099
pH will always increase the amount of

00:03:18.099 --> 00:03:19.810
uncharged acid that will penetrate the

00:03:19.810 --> 00:03:21.940
skin, and the pH required to get a high

00:03:21.940 --> 00:03:24.099
concentration of uncharged acid depends

00:03:24.099 --> 00:03:26.680
on which acid we're talking about. Why

00:03:26.680 --> 00:03:29.200
not use a super low pH then? You'd be

00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:30.819
correct in thinking that if we used a

00:03:30.819 --> 00:03:32.889
really low pH that would give us the

00:03:32.889 --> 00:03:34.660
maximum amount of uncharged acid that

00:03:34.660 --> 00:03:37.630
could penetrate. Unfortunately, skin likes

00:03:37.630 --> 00:03:40.449
to be at around pH 5 and very low pH can

00:03:40.449 --> 00:03:41.950
cause chemical burns.

00:03:41.950 --> 00:03:44.230
For leave-on products, it's recommended that

00:03:44.230 --> 00:03:46.959
you use alpha hydroxy acids at a pH of

00:03:46.959 --> 00:03:49.000
less than four and a concentration

00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:51.670
between 4 and 10 percent, and ascorbic

00:03:51.670 --> 00:03:54.640
acid at a pH of around 3.5 and a

00:03:54.640 --> 00:03:56.410
concentration between 5 and 20 percent.

00:03:58.240 --> 00:04:01.300
What about the other acids? It gets more

00:04:01.300 --> 00:04:03.070
complex here because the rest of the

00:04:03.070 --> 00:04:04.630
acid molecule makes a big difference.

00:04:04.630 --> 00:04:07.600
Hyaluronic acid is a large polymer made

00:04:07.600 --> 00:04:09.610
up of thousands of these units, so it's

00:04:09.610 --> 00:04:11.410
way too big to get into the skin anyway.

00:04:11.410 --> 00:04:14.020
And it doesn't need to - it's a humectant

00:04:14.020 --> 00:04:16.060
moisturizer which means it sits on top

00:04:16.060 --> 00:04:17.950
of skin and holds on to water to keep

00:04:17.950 --> 00:04:21.100
your skin moist. For retinoic acid, the

00:04:21.100 --> 00:04:22.870
rest of the molecule is non-polar enough

00:04:22.870 --> 00:04:24.280
that the charge doesn't make a big

00:04:24.280 --> 00:04:26.980
difference. For salicylic acid or beta

00:04:26.980 --> 00:04:29.320
hydroxy acid it's often recommended that

00:04:29.320 --> 00:04:30.940
you use a product with a pH of less than

00:04:30.940 --> 00:04:33.370
3.5 and a concentration of 1-2%

00:04:33.370 --> 00:04:35.320
but there's some evidence that

00:04:35.320 --> 00:04:37.240
it's also non-polar enough that pH

00:04:37.240 --> 00:04:39.460
doesn't matter. That's a quick

00:04:39.460 --> 00:04:41.110
introduction to pH and acids in

00:04:41.110 --> 00:04:42.880
skincare. I definitely haven't covered

00:04:42.880 --> 00:04:44.350
everything because delivering things

00:04:44.350 --> 00:04:49.660
through skin is a huge huge topic. I hope

00:04:49.660 --> 00:04:51.490
you found this video useful, and if you'd

00:04:51.490 --> 00:04:52.900
like to see more videos like this from

00:04:52.900 --> 00:04:54.460
me in the future, please comment and let

00:04:54.460 --> 00:04:56.580
me know!

